mcqs.question:
A component's state is `{ count: 0 }`. One click handler runs:

setCount(count + 1);
setCount(count + 1);
setCount(count + 1);

What is `count` in the next render?

code.code:
function SearchResults({ query }: { query: string }) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState<{ id: string; title: string }[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`)
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((data) => setResults(data));
  }, [query]);

  return <ul>{results.map((r) => <li key={r.id}>{r.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The dependency list is not the bug: `[query]` is exactly what the Effect reads, and 'fixing' the deps would be a misdiagnosis. The defect is that the Effect does not own its request lifetime. Sequence: the user types quickly, so the Effect for query 'a' starts fetch A; then query changes to 'ab', React runs the previous setup's cleanup (which does nothing, because none exists) and runs the new setup, starting fetch B. If A is slower and resolves after B, `setResults(A)` overwrites the newer results for 'ab'. The same hole wastes updates after unmount. There is also no error handling at all. The fix is one request per setup, torn down in cleanup: create an AbortController in setup, abort it in cleanup, and filter the expected AbortError out of the catch so real failures still surface.

useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then((data) => setResults(data))
    .catch((error) => {
      if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
        // surface real failures, e.g. setError(error)
      }
    });
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [query]);

An alternative is an `ignore` flag captured per setup (`let ignore = false; return () => { ignore = true; }`, then `if (!ignore) setResults(data)`), which prevents the stale write but does not cancel the network or server work. For production server data, prefer a framework loader or a cache/query layer that provides deduplication, freshness, and cancellation semantics. Sources: https://react.dev/learn/synchronizing-with-effects, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController.

